@@ -51,6 +51,8 @@ The same experimental literature warns against the obvious alternative — buyin
 
 Recent work on recursive self-improvement supports the structural form of that answer. Kim et al. (2026) observe that systems which edit themselves must leave part of their own editing machinery untouched to stay stable, capping the meta-depth they realize at roughly two, and respond by holding the meta-operation fixed and recursing on its input instead: because the operation never changes it cannot destabilize the system, and depth is set by convergence rather than fixed in advance. That is the immutable core's logic in miniature — the one component never improved is what lets everything below it improve without a fixed ceiling — demonstrated over benchmark runs rather than megayears.
 
+The bound on "almost unlimited" is quantitative, and it sits in the per-generation modification budget rather than in the choice of adaptation method. Azancort Neto et al. (2026) ran recursive fine-tuning on synthetic data across three architectures and up to ten generations and mapped the outcome onto three regimes: homeostatic, bounded, and degradative. The transition between them is threshold-like in perturbation magnitude, with the boundary falling between effective ranks 50 and 88 for one architecture and between 3 and 6 for another, a tenfold difference; halving synthetic exposure moved one system toward the bounded regime, worth 9.2 percentage points of factual retention by the tenth generation. The result that matters most here is a dissociation they report at intermediate capacity: factual retention held near 90% while output-distribution quality collapsed threefold. A core-integrity check that samples stated values and finds them intact can therefore pass a lineage that is already degrading, which is why verification has to test behavior under load rather than recitation.
+
 What the proof-before-adoption requirement must consist of is not a formality, and the obvious implementation of it fails. Wang et al. (2026) show that the acceptance rule actually used by self-evolving agents — keep any edit that raises an aggregate validation score — admits permanent regressions, because an edit can lift the average while breaking items the system already handled, and is biased upward besides by the Optimizer's Curse, since the best score observed on a finite noisy validation set overstates an edit's true value; their remedy is per-item paired comparison on identical validation items, plus a one-sided test that commits an edit only when its wins are statistically reliable and abstains otherwise. The setting is benchmark skill documents rather than a megayear cognitive layer, but for a probe with no external evaluator the asymmetry is the whole point: a regression accepted once is inherited by every descendant, so the gate must be conservative by construction rather than merely favorable in expectation.
 
 Tayebati et al. (2026) supply the runtime form of such a gate, and a measure of how much it catches: an agent proposes changes to its own operating instructions, and an external runtime gate grants them provisional authority during evaluation but persistent cross-episode authority only after measured improvement on the triggering failure without regression beyond a fixed margin on a set of protected cases. Of 383 proposals the gate decided by replay, 211 — 55% — repaired the failure that prompted them while breaking a case that had previously worked, and task-completion score was higher with the gate than without it in all sixteen matched runs across three agent benchmarks. The horizon is a benchmark episode rather than a megayear, but the result names what the interpretive layer's higher validation threshold must actually test: local proof of benefit is not proof of benefit, and a self-modification is admissible only against a protected set the change was not allowed to touch.
